  <page>
    <title>Virtual storage</title>
    <id>41853</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39505659</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-13T23:48:55Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Donhalcon</username>
        <id>392180</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>(stub-sort)</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Virtual storage''' is the [[computer storage|storage]] space that may be regarded as addressable [[main storage]] by the [[user]] of a [[computer system]] in which virtual addresses are mapped into real addresses. 

The size of virtual storage is limited by the addressing scheme of the computer system and by the amount of [[auxiliary storage]] available, and not by the actual number of main storage locations.

==Types==
There are two main ways of implementing [[virtual storage]].
*In-band where the management happens in a box wich sits between the application boxes and the storage devices. This has the drawback of a single point of failure, added latency and reduced scalability.
*Out-of-band isn't in the connection chain but there is a server wich sends information wich storage is where. To make this work there needs to be a piece of software on all connecting application boxes. This adds complexity and extra management of the client software.

==See also==
*[[Virtual memory]]

[[Category:Computer file systems]]
{{compu-storage-stub}}</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Virtual terminal</title>
    <id>41854</id>
    <revision>
      <id>35865235</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-19T22:00:50Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>128.101.154.66</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>Added description of unix-like OS use of virtual terminals</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[Open system (computing)|open systems]], a '''virtual terminal''' ('''VT''') is an [[application service]] that:
# Allows [[Server|host]] terminals on a multi-[[user]] [[computer network|network]] to interact with other hosts regardless of [[computer terminal|terminal]] type and characteristics,
# Allows remote log-on by local area network managers for the purpose of management,
# Allows users to [[access]] [[information]] from another host [[central processing unit|processor]] for transaction processing,
# Serves as a backup [[facility]].

[[ITU-T]] defines a virtual terminal protocol based on the [[Open Systems Interconnection|OSI]] [[application layer]] protocols. However, the virtual terminal protocol is not widely used on the Internet.

Some [[Unix-like]] operating systems use virtual terminal to describe the distinct login prompts offered by a single [[Computer console|console]].  Switching is performed with key combo like Ctrl-Alt-F1, Ctrl-Alt-F7, or Alt-F1.  Frequently the F7 terminal is used by an [[X11]] server for a [[GUI]] environment.  [[GNU Screen]] offers similar multiplexing features, but is initiated after login.

==Sources==
*[[Federal Standard 1037C]]

==See also==
*[[Terminal emulator]]

[[Category:ITU-T recommendations]]

[[de:Virtual Terminal]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Voice frequency</title>
    <id>41855</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41043404</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-24T18:08:47Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Deville</username>
        <id>364144</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Disambiguate [[Audio]] to [[Sound]] using [[:en:Wikipedia:Tools/Navigation_popups|popups]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">A '''voice frequency''' (VF) or '''voice band''' is one of the frequencies, within part of the [[Sound|audio]] range, that is used for the [[transmission (telecommunications)|transmission]] of [[speech]].  

In [[telephony]], the usable voice frequency [[band (radio)|band]] ranges from approximately 300 Hz to 3400 Hz. The [[bandwidth]] allocated for a single voice-frequency transmission [[channel (communications)|channel]] is usually 4 kHz, including [[guard band]]s, allowing a [[sample rate]] of 8 kHz to be used as the basis of the [[pulse code modulation]] system used for the digital [[PSTN]].

The term ''voice frequency'' can also be used to refer to the band of the [[electromagnetic spectrum]] between 300 and 3000 Hz.

== Fundamental frequency ==

The [[Human voice|voiced speech]] of a typical adult male will have a [[fundamental frequency]] of from 85 to 155 Hz, and that of a typical adult female from 165 to 255 Hz. Thus, the fundamental frequency of most speech falls below the bottom of the &quot;voice frequency&quot; band as defined above. However, enough of the [[harmonic series (music)|harmonic series]] will be present for the [[missing fundamental]] to create the impression of hearing the fundamental tone.

== References ==
* Baken, R. J. (1987). ''Clinical Measurement of Speech and Voice.'' London: Taylor and Francis Ltd. 

== See also ==
* [[formant]]

''An earlier version of this article contains public domain text originally from [[Federal Standard 1037C]] in support of [[MIL-STD-188]].''

[[Category:Human voice]]

[[pl:VF (fale)]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Voice frequency primary patch bay</title>
    <id>41856</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40190197</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-18T21:20:16Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Deville</username>
        <id>364144</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Disambiguate [[Signal]] to [[Signalling (telecommunication)]] using [[:en:Wikipedia:Tools/Navigation_popups|popups]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[telecommunication]], a '''voice frequency primary patch bay''' (VF) is a patching [[facility]] that provides the first appearance of local-[[user]] VF circuits in the [[technical control facility]] (TCF).  

The VF primary [[patch bay]] provides patching, [http://wiktionary.org/wiki/Transwiki:Monitoring monitoring], and testing for all VF circuits. Signals will have various levels and [[Signalling (telecommunication)|signaling]] schemes depending on the user [[terminal equipment]].

{{com-stub}}</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
