  <page>
    <title>Weser River</title>
    <id>41885</id>
    <revision>
      <id>40225632</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-19T02:06:38Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Alureiter</username>
        <id>401877</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Infobox_river | river_name = Weser
  | image_name = Weser2.JPG
  | caption = The Weser near Bad Oeynhausen.
  | origin = [[Fulda River|Fulda]] and [[Werra]]
  | mouth = [[North Sea]]
  | basin_countries = [[Germany]]
  | length = 452 km
  | elevation = 117 m
  | discharge = 
  | watershed = 46,306 km²
}}
[[image:Weser watershed 7.png|thumb|Weser watershed]]
[[Image:Orthographic projection centred over Bremen and the Weser watershed.png|thumb|Orthographic projection centred over Bremen]]

The '''Weser''' is a [[river]] of north-western [[Germany]]. Formed at [[Hannoversch Muenden|Hannoversch Münden]] by the confluence of the [[Fulda River|Fulda]] and [[Werra]] rivers, it flows through [[Bremen (city)|Bremen]] to the [[North Sea]], and has a length of 452 km. Together with the Werra, its length is 744 km.

The top section of the river's course leads through a hilly region called the [[Weserbergland]]. It extends from the confluence of Fulda and Werra to the [[Porta Westfalica]], where the Weser runs through a gorge between two mountain chains, the [[Wiehengebirge]] in the west and the [[Wesergebirge]] in the east.

Between [[Minden]] and the North Sea, the Weser has largely been [[ship canal|canalised]], permitting ships of up to 1,200 tons to navigate the river. Eight hydroelectric dams are located along its length. Further downstream, the river is linked to the Dortmund-Ems canal via the Küstenkanal, and another canal links the river at [[Bremerhaven]] to the [[Elbe River]]. A large reservoir on the [[Eder]] river, the main tributary of the Fulda, is used to regulate water levels on the Weser so as to ensure adequate depth for shipping throughout the year.  The dam, built in 1914, was bombed and destroyed by British planes in February 1943, causing massive loss of life and destruction downstream, but was rebuilt within four months.  Today, the reservoir ([[Edersee]]) is a major summer resort area and provides substantial hydroelectricity.

Towns along the Weser, from the confluence of Werra and Fulda to the mouth are: [[Hannoversch Muenden|Hannoversch Münden]], [[Beverungen]], [[Hoexter|Höxter]], [[Holzminden]], [[Bodenwerder]], [[Hamelin]], [[Hessisch-Oldendorf]], [[Rinteln]], [[Vlotho]], [[Bad Oeynhausen]], [[Porta Westfalica]], [[Minden]], [[Petershagen]], [[Nienburg]], [[Achim]], [[Bremen (city)|Bremen]], [[Brake, Germany|Brake]], [[Nordenham]], [[Bremerhaven]].

The largest tributary of the Weser is the [[Aller]] river, which joins the Weser south of Bremen.

'''Tributaries''' of Weser and Werra (from source to mouth):
* Left
** [[Ulster River|Ulster]]
** [[Fulda]], with [[Eder]]
** [[Diemel]]
** [[Große Aue]]
** [[Hunte]]
* Right
** [[Nesse]]
** [[Aller]]
** [[Lesum|Lesum (Wümme)]]

[[image:AlteWeser 1964.jpg|thumb|left|[[Alte Weser (lighthouse)|Lighthouse ''Alte Weser'']]]]
The Weser enters the [[North Sea]] in the most southern part of the [[German Bight]]. In the North Sea it splits up into two arms representing the ancient riverbed at the end of the last [[ice age]]. These sea-arms are called ''Alte Weser'' (old Weser) and ''Neue Weser'' (new Weser). They represent the major waterways for ships heading for the harbors of [[Bremerhaven]], [[Nordenham]] and [[Bremen (city)|Bremen]]. The most northern site of the Weser is marked by the [[Alte Weser (lighthouse)|lighthouse ''Alte Weser'']]. This lighthouse replaced the historical and famous lighthouse ''[[Roter Sand]]'' in the year 1964.


