  <page>
    <title>Gain</title>
    <id>41968</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41052187</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-24T19:17:10Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Stephenb</username>
        <id>292494</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>Revert to revision 40201712 using [[:en:Wikipedia:Tools/Navigation_popups|popups]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">''For other uses, see [[Gain (disambiguation)]].''

In [[electronics]], '''gain''' is usually taken as the mean [[ratio]] of the [[Signalling (telecommunication)|signal output]] of a system to the [[Signalling (telecommunication)|signal input]] of the system.  A gain of five would imply that either the [[voltage]] or [[Electric power|power]] is increased by a factor of five. It has wide application in [[electronic amplifier|amplifiers]].

== Logarithmic units and Decibels ==

In electronics, it is common to use [[logarithmic units]] to measure gain. Originally, the [[decibel|bel]] was used:

Gain = log&lt;sub&gt;10&lt;/sub&gt;
(''P''&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;/''P''&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;) bel

where P1 and P2 are the input and output ''powers'' respectively. 

This unit is generally too large, so the [[decibel]] (one tenth of a bel) became popular in its place. As there are ten [[decibel]]s ('''dB''') in a bel:

Gain = 10 * log&lt;sub&gt;10&lt;/sub&gt;
(''P''&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;/''P''&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;) dB

(A similar unit using [[natural logarithm]]s is called the [[neper]].)

When gain is calculated using voltage instead of power, making the substitution [[Joule's law|(''P''=''V''&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;/''R'')]], the formula is: 

*Gain = 10 * log ((''V''&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;/''R'') /(''V''&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;/''R'') ) dB

*Gain = 10 * log ((''V''&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;/''V''&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;)&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; ) dB

*Gain = 20 * log (''V''&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;/''V''&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;) dB

This formula only holds true if the load [[impedance]]s are identical. In many modern electronic devices, output impedances are low enough and input impedances high enough that load can be ignored without significantly affecting the calculation.

=== Example ===

If an amplifier produces an output of 1 volt into a 1 ohm load, then it is providing 1 watt of output power. If the amplifier is then altered to produce an output of 10 volts into the same load, it is now providing 100 watts of output power (''P''=''V''&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;/''R''). Therefore:

voltage gain = 10 times
&lt;br/&gt;
power gain = 100 times

&amp;mdash; which by definition is said to be a gain of 20 dB.

A gain of factor 1 or (equivalent to 0 dB) where both input and output are at the same voltage level is also known as ''[[unity]] gain''.

==See also==
* [[Federal Standard 1037C]]
*[[Transmitter power output]]
*[[Absolute gain]]
*[[Loop gain]]
*[[Insertion gain]]
*[[Directive gain]]
*[[Signal processing gain]]
*[[Net gain]]
*[[Automatic gain control]]
*[[Loss]], [[Aperture-to-medium coupling loss]], [[Effective radiated power]]

[[fr:gain]]
[[ja:&amp;#21033;&amp;#24471;]]

[[Category:Antenna terminology]][[Category:Electronics terms]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Loss</title>
    <id>41969</id>
    <revision>
      <id>35602104</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-17T23:01:44Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Eadric</username>
        <id>17374</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Spelling correction</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{wiktionary}}

'''Loss''' has several meanings including:

* Loss in [[electronics]] is the ratio of the system output to system input
** In electronics, loss is the ratio of system output to system input. Calculation and units used are the same as with [[Gain]], except that the output is less than the input. Loss is often expressed as a negative gain (if expressed in [[decibel|dB]]) or as a gain of less than one.
** For example: if ''input''=100&amp;nbsp;mW and ''output''=5&amp;nbsp;mW then
**''loss'' = 5/100 = 0.05 = &amp;minus;1.3&amp;nbsp;B ([[bel]]) or &amp;minus;13&amp;nbsp;dB
* Loss in [[insurance]] is the reduction in economical [[value#Economics|value]] after an event occurs. It can take the form of a [[property]] or a casualty loss.
* Loss in [[finance]] is the reduction in economical [[value#Economics|value]] of an [[asset]] or the [[mark-to-market]] of a derivative.
* In accounting, a loss in is the reduction in the fair (usually defined as market) value of an asset or increase in the fair value of a liability due to an event that is not related to the earnings process.  As such, it is the opposite of a “gain”.
* Slang for someone not liked very much.
* ''[[Loss (album)|Loss]]'', the debut album of [[Colin MacIntyre|Mull Historical Society]].
* For loss as the [[pitcher|pitching]] statistic in [[baseball]], see [[Win (baseball)]].

{{disambig}}

[[de:Verlust]]
[[es:Loser]]
[[fr:Perte]]
[[io:Perdajo]]</text>
