      <text xml:space="preserve">{{dablink|There is another '''Wilhelm Grimm''' who was a [[Nazi]] leader. See: [[Nazi Party leaders and officials]].}}

[[Image:1000-DEM-OBV-178x83.jpg|200px|thumb|The Brothers Grimm on a 1000DM banknote.]]

'''Wilhelm Carl Grimm''' ([[February 24]], [[1786]] &amp;ndash; [[December 16]] [[1859]]) was a German author, one of the [[Brothers Grimm]].

He was born in [[Hanau]], [[Germany]] and in [[1803]] he started studying [[law]] at the University of [[Marburg]], one year after his brother [[Jacob Grimm|Jacob]] did the same.

In [[1825]] Wilhelm married Henriette Dorothea Wild, also known as ''Dortchen''.

From [[1837]]-[[1841]] the Grimm Brothers joined five of their colleague professors at the [[University of Göttingen]] to form a group known as the ''Göttingen Sieben'' (''The Göttingen Seven''). They protested against [[Ernst August]], King of [[Hanover]], whom they accused of violating the [[constitution]]. All seven were fired by the [[monarch|king]].

