      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>/* Notable Montpelier Residents */  slight alphabeticalization</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Montpelier vt state house 20040420.jpg|thumb|left|220px|[[Vermont State House]], Vermont's third on this site, built in 1859]]
{{Infobox City |official_name = Montpelier, Vermont
|nickname=
|image_flag = MontpelierVT.jpg
|image_seal = MontpelierVTseal.gif 
|image_map = montpelier_vt_highlight.png
|mapsize=150px
|map_caption = Location in [[Vermont]]
|subdivision_type = [[Countries of the world|Country]]&lt;br&gt;  [[Political divisions of the United States|State]]&lt;br&gt;   [[List of counties in Vermont|County]]
|subdivision_name = [[United States]]&lt;br&gt;[[Vermont]]&lt;br&gt;[[Washington County, Vermont|Washington County]]
|leader_title = [[Mayor]]
|leader_name = Mary Hooper
|area_magnitude = 1 E8 
|area_total = 10.3 sq. miles / 26.6
|area_land = 10.2 sq. miles / 26.5
|area_water = 0.1 sq. miles / 0.1
|population_as_of = 2000
|population_note =
|population_total = 8,035 (city proper) 
|population_density = 302.7
|timezone = [[Eastern Standard Time|EST]]
|utc_offset = -5
|timezone_DST = [[Eastern Daylight Time|EDT]]
|utc_offset_DST = -4
|latitude = 44° 15' 59'' N
|longitude = 72° 34' 18.98'' W
|latd=44 |latm=15 |lats=59 |latNS=N
|longd=72 |longm=34 |longs=18.98 |longEW=W
|website = [http://www.montpelier-vt.org www.montpelier-vt.org]
|footnotes = }}
'''Montpelier''' is the capital of the [[U.S. state]] of [[Vermont]] and  the [[county seat]] of [[Washington County, Vermont|Washington County]]. Known for its lively downtown driven by the activity of state government, Montpelier is the site of the [[Vermont State House]], visible from the outskirts of town, is located on State Street on the west side of downtown. The [[Winooski River]] flows along  the south edge of downtown and is fed by several smaller tributaries that cut through residential districts. 

Along with [[Barre (town), Vermont|Barre]], the city forms a small [[metropolitian area]] in the center of the state. As of the [[United States Census, 2000|2000 Census]], it was the least-populous [[state capital]], with a population of 8035. It is famously the only state capital without a [[McDonald's Corporation|McDonald's]] [[franchising|franchise]], and home to the last remaining [[clothespin]] [[manufacturer]] in the [[United States]]. Processing [[granite]], mainly from the [[quarry|quarries]] in nearby [[Barre (town), Vermont|Barre]] is a major part of the city's economic activity; [[timber]] was once a large industry in the region in the [[19th century]] but has largely declined. The city is also home to the [[New England Culinary Institute]] and the headquarters offices of several insurance companies.

== Geography ==
According to the [[United States Census Bureau]], the city has a total area of 26.6 [[square kilometre|km&amp;sup2;]] (10.3 [[square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]]).  26.5 km&amp;sup2; (10.2 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and 0.10% is water.

== Demographics ==
[[Image:DSCN4162 montpelierbearpondbooks e.jpg|250px|left|thumb|Montpelier has a lively downtown with coffeeshops and bookshops, giving it somewhat of a [[counterculture]] feel]]
As of the [[census]][[Geographic references#2|&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;]] of [[2000]], there are 8,035 people, 3,739 households, and 1,940 families residing in the city.  The [[population density]] is 302.7/km&amp;sup2; (784.0/mi&amp;sup2;).  There are 3,899 housing units at an average density of 146.9/km&amp;sup2; (380.4/mi&amp;sup2;).  The racial makeup of the city is 96.55% [[White (U.S. Census)|White]], 0.65% [[African American (U.S. Census)|African American]], 0.24% [[Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]], 0.82% [[Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]], 0.01% [[Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander]], 0.39% from [[Race (U.S. Census)|other races]], and 1.34% from two or more races.  1.41% of the population are [[Hispanic (U.S. Census)|Hispanic]] or [[Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 3,739 households out of which 26.0% have children under the age of 18 [[year]]s living with them, 38.5% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 10.1% have a female householder with no husband present, and 48.1% are non-families. 39.4% of all households are made up of individuals and 13.1% have someone living alone who is 65 [[year]]s of age or older.  The average household size is 2.09 and the average family size is 2.84.

In the city the population is spread out with 21.3% under the age of 18, 8.6% from 18 to 24, 28.2% from 25 to 44, 27.1% from 45 to 64, and 14.9% who are 65 [[year]]s of age or older.  The median age is 40 [[year]]s.  For every 100 females there are 84.2 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 82.0 males.

The median income for a household in the city is $37,513, and the median income for a family is $51,818. Males have a median income of $35,957 versus $29,442 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the city is $22,599.  9.8% of the population and 7.2% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 12.9% of those under the age of 18 and 5.7% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

==Notable Montpelier Residents==
*[[William Charles Fitzgerald]]
*[[George Dewey]], admiral of the [[US Navy]].
*[[Patrick Leahy]], US senator for [[Vermont]].
*[[Arthur E. Scott]], US Senate's first photo-historian.
*[[John Thurston]], US Senator for [[Nebraska]].
*[[Samuel C. Upham]], journalist.

== External links ==
