  <page>
    <title>Trace radioisotope</title>
    <id>42021</id>
    <revision>
      <id>32842066</id>
      <timestamp>2005-12-27T04:54:00Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Brian Sisco</username>
        <id>297807</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>rv</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">A '''trace radioisotope''' is a [[radioisotope]] that is naturally occurring. This natural formation can be from the decay of heavier nuclei such as [[uranium]]-235 decaying into [[cesium]]-135. Natural occurrence of radioisotopes can also be driven by [[cosmic ray]]s. This is the method that creates [[hydrogen-3]]. Isotopes with [[half-life|half-live]]s greater than about 80 million years also remain in trace amounts from the formation of the Earth. [[Potassium]]-40 and [[vanadium]]-50 fit into this category. 

{{chem-stub}}

